From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] aic8800: give the boot-path prints a log level

Measured on the board, 2026-09-17, a normal boot
(`dmesg` err/warn section): 67 lines from aic_load_fw and aic8800_fdrv sit at
KERN_WARNING on every boot, and none of them reports a fault. 63 are bare
printk() with no KERN_ level, which the kernel emits at the default message
level, 4 = KERN_WARNING (the chain is in the header of aic8800-lower-the-default-log-level). The other 4 are
AICWFDBG(LOGERROR, ...) for values that are not errors. That patch's own
observable reads correctly on the same boot: no AICWFDBG(LOGINFO) line at
all, so the SSID call sites (all LOGINFO or LOGDEBUG) stay quiet.

In the vendor driver: progress chatter and identity lines share one
level with real failures, so `journalctl -p warning` cannot tell a dead
radio from a working one; and LOGERROR, the one gate bit that stays on, is
used for a firmware memory readout and the P2P device address.

What changes, by the line in that log. No message text changes, no line is
deleted:

  pr_info (identity and proof lines, still in dmesg by default)
    aic_load_fw  RELEASE DATE (aic_bluetooth_main.c)          1
                 Aic <speed> speed USB device detected         1
                 chip_id=, chip_mcu_id = (aic_compat_8800d80.c) 1
                 aic_load_firmware :firmware path =            5
                 file md5: (the pinned-hash proof, README)     5
                 ### Upload ... firmware / fw_patch_table      5
                 fw download complete                          4
                 patch version                                 1
                 aicwf_usb_disconnect enter                    1
  pr_debug (progress chatter, on with dynamic debug)
    aic_load_fw  aic_bluetooth_mod_init                        1
                 addr_adid ..., addr_patch ...                 1
                 aicbt_patch_table_load bt <6 values>          6
                 app_cmp (aicwf_usb_rx_submit_all_urb)        20
                 aicwf_bus_deinit / exit aicwf_bus_deinit      2
                 q_sz/max (cmd_mgr_print)                      2
                 usb bustx/busrx thread will to stop / stop    4
    aic8800_fdrv ========== tcp ack debug tcp_ack_init         1
                 rx eapol (rwnx_rxdataind_aicwf)               2
  AICWFDBG LOGERROR -> LOGINFO
    aic_load_fw  Read FW mem: and its two addr=value lines     3
    aic8800_fdrv p2p dev addr=                                 1

Only lines that a normal boot and association print were touched. The
failure prints next to them (rd fail, upload fail, file failed to open,
wrong size) keep their bare printk and so stay at KERN_WARNING, and so does
rwnx_send_reboot's "enter" line, which the README names as an owed
observable. Radxa's own fix-Lower-the-debugging-log-level.patch does
not reach any of these lines.

Switching the quiet lines back on, no rebuild (CONFIG_DYNAMIC_DEBUG is
pinned in A733_CONFIGS), scoped by function:
  echo 'module aic_load_fw func aicbt_patch_table_load +p' \
    > /sys/kernel/debug/dynamic_debug/control
  aic_load_fw.dyndbg="func aicwf_usb_rx_submit_all_urb +p"   (command line)
The four LOGINFO lines print at firmware upload and interface creation, so
their gate has to be open at module load, and each module has its own:
  aic_load_fw.aicwf_dbg_level=3 aic8800_fdrv.aicwf_dbg_level=3  (command line)
or the same as `options` lines in modprobe.d. ⚠ LOGINFO also opens the
SSID call sites, so such a log is not publishable unredacted.

aicwf_usb_disconnect enter stays at pr_info on purpose: it is the one line
from this driver when the radio leaves the bus, expected or not (it prints
once per boot at the firmware re-enumeration). The bus-deinit and thread-stop
lines that follow it go to pr_debug.

Falsifier, on the board after a normal boot with WLAN associated:
1. `journalctl -k -b -p warning` holds no line from either module (compare
   the 67 in the log above).
2. `journalctl -k -b -p info` still holds the firmware path, md5, Upload
   and patch version lines, one set per boot.
3. The dyndbg line for aicbt_patch_table_load, then a reload of the loader,
   prints the six bt values.
4. WLAN associates and Bluetooth comes up as before (hci0 UP RUNNING).

Assisted-by: Claude

